<section class="hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">NEXORA · NXL</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Welcome to <nxl expr="app_name" /></h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A generated NXL web application: every page is a server-rendered
    <code>.nxlhtml</code> template, every route is declared in
    <code>nxl.toml</code>, and every value is escaped on the way out.
  </p>
  <p class="actions">
    <a class="btn" href="/about">Get started</a>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/contact">Contact</a>
  </p>
</section>

<ul class="cards">
  <li class="panel">
    <h2>Declared routes</h2>
    <p>
      <code>nxl.toml</code> lists every route, the page it renders and the
      fields a form must supply. The server serves exactly that table —
      nothing more.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li class="panel">
    <h2>Escaped output</h2>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;nxl expr&gt;</code> escapes its output, so a value can never
      become markup. <a href="/search">Search for a term</a> to see the page
      echo it safely.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li class="panel">
    <h2>The language itself</h2>
    <p>
      <code>src/</code> is real NXL — modules, classes and arrays — compiled
      and run by the toolchain, not a scripting shim around another
      language.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>How routing works here</h2>
  <p>
    <code>nxl.toml</code> declares the routes. Each one names a page under
    <code>pages/</code>, and <code>nexora serve</code> renders it.
  </p>
  <ul class="list">
    <li><a href="/about">A static page</a></li>
    <li><a href="/posts/1">A path parameter</a></li>
    <li><a href="/contact">A form with validation</a></li>
    <li><a href="/does-not-exist">A real 404</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>Escaping</h2>
  <p>
    <nxl expr='"<b>this is escaped</b>"' /> renders as
    <code><nxl expr='"&lt;b&gt;this is escaped&lt;/b&gt;"' /></code>, because
    <code>expr</code> escapes its output. A value can never become markup
    through it.
  </p>
</section>
